(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-pleural-disorders-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pleural Disorders Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A first primary spontaneous pneumothorax has an apex-to-cupola distance greater than 3 cm with significant dyspnea and chest pain. Which treatment is listed?","choices":["Thoracoscopic pleurodesis","Open thoracotomy","Chest tube insertion","Observation with oxygen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A moderate or large primary spontaneous pneumothorax, more than 3 cm at the apex, with moderate or severe symptoms such as significant chest pain and dyspnea, is treated with chest tube insertion. A recurrent primary pneumothorax instead goes to thoracoscopic surgery with pleurodesis. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 50, 53, 55"},{"q":"What does thoracentesis typically show in chylothorax?","choices":["Frank pus on aspiration","Bloody fluid, high hematocrit","Clear, straw-colored fluid","Milky fluid, high triglycerides"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thoracentesis in chylothorax shows milky fluid with elevated triglycerides, reflecting lipid from chyle that has leaked through a disrupted thoracic duct. On the pleural fluid color guide, milky white means chylothorax. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 74, 98, 104, 105, 108"},{"q":"A pleural fluid has a protein ratio to serum of 0.7, and both lactate dehydrogenase criteria are unmet. How is it classified?","choices":["Exudate","Transudate","Indeterminate","Normal pleural fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Light's criteria classify a fluid as an exudate when at least one criterion is met, and a protein ratio of 0.7 is above the 0.5 cutoff. The two unmet lactate dehydrogenase criteria do not change that, because a transudate requires all of the criteria to be absent. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 5, 101, 110, 112"},{"q":"Which respiratory rate is part of the American College of Chest Physicians definition of a stable patient with spontaneous pneumothorax?","choices":["Below 20 breaths per minute","Below 24 breaths per minute","Below 30 breaths per minute","Below 36 breaths per minute"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The American College of Chest Physicians calls a patient stable only with all of: respiratory rate under 24 breaths per minute, heart rate above 60 and below 120, normal blood pressure, room-air oxygen saturation above 90 percent and the ability to speak in whole sentences. All other patients are unstable. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 51, 54"},{"q":"Which underlying condition most commonly causes a secondary spontaneous pneumothorax?","choices":["Interstitial lung disease","Connective tissue disease","Bronchogenic carcinoma","Chronic obstructive lung disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, also called chronic obstructive lung disease, is the most common underlying abnormality in secondary spontaneous pneumothorax, through rupture of apical blebs. Lung malignancy, cystic fibrosis, pneumonia, tuberculosis, sarcoidosis and Marfan syndrome are other causes. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 17, 20"},{"q":"What is the most common cause of hemothorax?","choices":["Trauma","Neoplasm","Anticoagulation","Spontaneous pneumothorax"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Trauma is the most common cause of hemothorax, through injury to the heart or vasculature, the lung parenchyma, or the intercostal, pulmonary or internal thoracic arteries; blunt or penetrating trauma causes the majority of cases. Neoplasms are the other listed etiology. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 69, 74"},{"q":"Which radiographic sign suggests a pneumothorax on a supine film?","choices":["Straight air-fluid line","Deep sulcus sign","Blunted costophrenic angle","Stratosphere sign"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With the patient supine, pleural air collects laterally and makes the costophrenic angle dark and deep; that lucency is the deep sulcus sign. On an upright film the same air would instead show as a visceral pleural line. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 29, 34, 71, 90"},{"q":"Which is a risk factor for an infection-related pleural effusion?","choices":["Poor dental hygiene","Peritoneal dialysis","Thoracic surgery","Heart failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The listed risk factors for infection-related pleural effusion are diabetes, alcoholism, immunosuppression and poor dental hygiene. Infection is the second largest group of effusion causes after congestive heart failure. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 82, 83, 105"},{"q":"Catheter aspiration is contraindicated in a symptomatic pneumothorax from chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Which treatment is used instead?","choices":["Needle decompression","Analgesia and discharge","Chest tube insertion","Observation with oxygen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Underlying pulmonary disease is a contraindication to catheter aspiration, and most secondary spontaneous pneumothoraces are treated with chest tube insertion; a small secondary one gets a chest tube once it causes symptoms. Thoracoscopic surgery, thoracotomy or chemical pleurodesis via the tube can follow. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 20, 39, 56, 57, 66"},{"q":"Pleurisy is inflammation of which structure?","choices":["The intercostal muscles","The parietal pleura","The visceral pleura","The pericardium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pleurisy is inflammation of the parietal pleura: the pleural layers rub together and stimulate the pain fibers of the parietal pleura, causing pleuritic pain. It is a symptom rather than a diagnosis, so the condition underneath still has to be found and treated. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 9, 12, 13, 116"},{"q":"When counseling a patient after a first spontaneous pneumothorax, what recurrence risk within three years should be quoted?","choices":["About 5%","About 10%","About 50%","About 75%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patient education after a spontaneous pneumothorax explains the condition and warns that recurrence approaches 50% within three years after an initial episode. The best preventive procedure is video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery with pleurodesis, and smoking cessation should be discussed. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 59, 60"},{"q":"What does pleurodesis do to the pleural space?","choices":["Obliterates it through adhesions","Makes extra pleural fluid","Lets the layers glide freely","Drains it continuously"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pleurodesis obliterates the pleural space by inducing intrapleural inflammation: an irritant causes adhesions of the two pleural layers. That keeps the lung inflated after a pneumothorax and leaves no room for additional fluid to accumulate in a pleural effusion. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 41, 45, 46"},{"q":"How is uncomplicated pneumomediastinum managed after the airway is secured?","choices":["Needle decompression","Conservatively","Emergent thoracotomy","Chest tube insertion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After the airway is assessed and maintained and any identified cause is treated, pneumomediastinum is managed conservatively: the air is often gradually absorbed, oxygen may increase absorption, and the patient avoids physical activity and takes analgesics. A severe case gets an emergent cardiothoracic surgery consult. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 41, 66, 79"},{"q":"In a tension pneumothorax, what is done immediately if a chest tube is not readily available?","choices":["Catheter aspiration","Chest radiograph first","Heimlich valve","Needle decompression"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Immediate chest tube placement is ideal for a tension pneumothorax; if a chest tube is not readily available, immediate needle decompression is performed, followed by a moderate or large-size chest tube and admission. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 40, 65, 66, 67"},{"q":"Which feature separates a primary from a secondary spontaneous pneumothorax?","choices":["Size under 3 cm","No underlying lung disease","No history of chest trauma","Onset while at rest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both kinds of spontaneous pneumothorax occur without thoracic trauma; the primary form has no underlying lung abnormality, while the secondary form has one, chronic obstructive lung disease most commonly. Primary patients usually have good pulmonary reserve. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 17, 19, 20, 21, 28"},{"q":"How does pleurodynia often present in children, compared with adults?","choices":["Pleuritic chest pain","Shoulder pain","Painless fever","Abdominal pain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pleurodynia brings acute onset of fever with pleuritic pain in adults or abdominal pain in children, in paroxysms of severe, knife-like pain lasting 15 to 30 minutes with sweating and tachypnea. The involved muscles are usually tender. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 11, 117"},{"q":"Which apex-to-cupola distance separates a small from a large pneumothorax in the American College of Chest Physicians system?","choices":["1 cm","2 cm","3 cm","5 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The American College of Chest Physicians defines pneumothorax size by the apex-to-cupola distance, with a distance under 3 cm called small and larger distances called large. That one number then sorts the treatment of primary and secondary spontaneous pneumothorax. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 18, 28, 53, 55"},{"q":"Which is an indication for video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery in pneumothorax?","choices":["A small first primary episode","Tension pneumothorax","Simple pneumomediastinum","Bilateral pneumothorax"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thoracoscopic surgery is indicated for recurrences of spontaneous pneumothorax, bilateral pneumothorax, failure of tube thoracostomy (the lung fails to expand or an air leak persists) and secondary pneumothorax, because of its high recurrence rates and the greater consequences of recurrence. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 49, 53, 54, 66, 79"},{"q":"When is chest computed tomography impractical for a pneumothorax?","choices":["When time is critical","When blebs are suspected","When size must be measured","When lung disease is suspected"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thoracic computed tomography is the best test for presence, size and location of a pneumothorax, but it is impractical in a time-critical situation. A tension pneumothorax, for example, is a clinical diagnosis with no time even for the X-ray. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 33, 66"},{"q":"Pleurisy of the central diaphragm refers pain to the ipsilateral shoulder or neck through which nerve fibers?","choices":["Intercostal nerve fibers","Vagus nerve fibers","Visceral pleural fibers","Phrenic nerve fibers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The parietal pleura on the central diaphragm is innervated by phrenic nerve fibers, so inflammation there may cause ipsilateral, same-side, shoulder or neck pain. Elsewhere the parietal pleura has intercostal fibers, which give well-localized, sharp chest wall pain. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 11"},{"q":"Which pneumothorax is a contraindication to catheter aspiration?","choices":["A first primary episode","Small primary with dyspnea","Primary with good reserve","Bilateral pneumothoraces"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Catheter aspiration is contraindicated in bilateral pneumothoraces, and also in traumatic, tension and recurrent pneumothorax, hemodynamic instability, underlying pulmonary disease and bleeding disorders. Its place is a small primary pneumothorax with significant dyspnea after analgesia. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 19, 39, 54"},{"q":"Needle decompression of a tension pneumothorax is also known as what?","choices":["Thoracentesis","Catheter aspiration","Needle thoracostomy","Tube thoracostomy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Needle decompression, also known as needle thoracostomy, is insertion of a needle into the pleural space to decompress a tension pneumothorax, typically in the 2nd intercostal space, over the 3rd rib, at the midclavicular line. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 38, 39, 41, 67, 96"},{"q":"What is the leading cause of pleural effusion overall?","choices":["Pneumonia","Cirrhosis","Heart failure","Malignancy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Congestive heart failure is the leading cause of pleural effusion, through increased capillary hydrostatic pressure, and it accounts for more than 90% of transudative effusions. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 81, 82, 83, 102, 111"},{"q":"In Light's criteria, pleural fluid lactate dehydrogenase above what fraction of the upper limit of normal serum lactate dehydrogenase marks an exudate?","choices":["Three-quarters","Two-thirds","One-half","One-third"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The third criterion is pleural fluid lactate dehydrogenase greater than two-thirds of the upper limit of normal serum lactate dehydrogenase. The other two are the protein ratio above 0.5 and the lactate dehydrogenase ratio above 0.6; meeting any one marks an exudate. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 5, 101, 112"},{"q":"When is a diagnostic thoracentesis performed?","choices":["Routinely after every chest film","A new effusion with no clinical cause","An effusion explained by heart failure","Before the first chest radiograph"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Diagnostic thoracentesis removes 50 to 100 mL of fluid for visualization and analysis and is performed when there is a new pleural effusion with no clinical cause. Therapeutic thoracentesis, by contrast, removes fluid to relieve symptoms. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 90, 95, 96, 97, 113"},{"q":"Which traumatic pneumothorax has a direct chest-wall wound that lets air into the pleural space during inhalation?","choices":["Open pneumothorax","Closed pneumothorax","Iatrogenic pneumothorax","Hemopneumothorax"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An open pneumothorax has a direct wound in the chest wall that allows air to enter the pleural space during inhalation and can lead to a sucking chest wound. A closed pneumothorax has no external wound: air enters from the lung through broken ribs or internal injuries. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 17, 61, 62, 71"},{"q":"Why does a secondary spontaneous pneumothorax need more immediate intervention than a primary one?","choices":["A larger volume of air","Diminished pulmonary reserve","Larger blebs at the apex","More often bilateral"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Secondary spontaneous pneumothorax is a more serious event because the underlying lung disease leaves diminished pulmonary reserve, which can be life-threatening and requires more immediate intervention. The symptoms are the same as in the primary form but generally greater. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 20, 24, 39, 49, 56, 57"},{"q":"In which age range does primary spontaneous pneumothorax classically occur?","choices":["20 to 40 years","40 to 60 years","60 to 80 years","Over 80 years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Primary spontaneous pneumothorax is classically a disease of tall, thin young males aged 20 to 40 years, with males affected more than females. Smoking is a significant risk factor, and these patients typically have good pulmonary reserve, so severe distress is not common. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 19, 20"},{"q":"Which imaging test is the diagnostic gold standard for pneumothorax?","choices":["Chest computed tomography","Upright chest radiograph","Lung ultrasound","Lateral decubitus film"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thoracic computed tomography is the diagnostic gold standard: it is the best modality for determining presence, size and location of a pneumothorax, assesses underlying disease, and detects blebs and bullae. It is impractical in a time-critical situation. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 30, 33, 34, 91, 95"},{"q":"Which disorder accounts for more than 90% of transudative pleural effusions?","choices":["Nephrotic syndrome","Peritoneal dialysis","Congestive heart failure","Cirrhosis with ascites"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Congestive heart failure accounts for more than 90% of transudative effusions, through increased capillary hydrostatic pressure. Cirrhosis with ascites, nephrotic syndrome and peritoneal dialysis are other listed causes; a transudate is low-protein fluid with all of Light's criteria absent. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 81, 82, 110, 111"}]};
